494 - solve the whole boat-speed problem
Let v be still-water speed in km/h. Upstream travel is actually possible
only for v > 3. Downstream speed is v+3, upstream speed v-3.
The complete time equation is 4/(v+3) + 3/(v-3) = 10/v.
Under v > 3 all denominators are nonzero and multiplication is legitimate.
Combining gives (7v-3)/(v^2-9) = 10/v,
7v^2-3v = 10v^2-90, hence 3v^2+3v-90 = 0.
Equivalently (v+6)(v-5) = 0. Algebraic candidates are -6 and 5.
Only v = 5 satisfies v > 3. Still-water speed is 5 km/h.
Check: 4/8 + 3/2 = 1/2 + 3/2 = 2 hours, equal to 10/5.
Task: build the direction-sensitive distance/speed time equation,
solve it, reject the inadmissible root and keep physical units.

495 - prove arithmetic progression and find its first term/difference
For every consecutive index n, x_(n+1)-x_n
 = [8-3(n+1)]-[8-3n] = -3.
This difference is independent of n, proving the arithmetic progression.
The first term, at n = 1 as explicitly requested, is x_1 = 8-3 = 5.
The common difference is -3, not 3; x_0 = 8 is not the first term.
Task: prove the universal consecutive-difference identity, not just
check a finite list, and report first term and common difference.

496 - prove geometric progression and find its first term/ratio
y_n = 64*(1/2)^n > 0, so the consecutive-term quotient is defined.
For every consecutive index n, y_(n+1)/y_n
 = [64*(1/2)^(n+1)]/[64*(1/2)^n] = 1/2.
This quotient is independent of n, proving the geometric progression.
At n = 1, y_1 = 32. The common ratio is 1/2 in the forward direction.
y_0 = 64 is not the requested first term; reversing the quotient gives 2
but is not the forward common ratio.
Task: prove the universal quotient identity with nonzero terms,
and report the first term and forward ratio.

497 - determine all three membership claims without choosing a zero convention
For natural indices, a_n = sqrt(n^2+5n) is a principal nonnegative root.
6: n^2+5n = 36 gives (n+9)(n-4) = 0. n = 4 is admissible,
   n = -9 is not a natural index. Thus 6 = a_4 is a term.
-6: impossible because every a_n is nonnegative; it is not a term.
5: n^2+5n = 25 has discriminant 125, not an integer square.
   Its positive real root (-5+5sqrt(5))/2 is not a natural number.
   Alternatively n^2+5n is strictly increasing for natural n;
   at n = 3 it is 24 < 25 and at n = 4 it is 36 > 25.
   Therefore no natural index yields 5.
If 0 is allowed, its only added term is a_0 = 0, which changes none of
these three conclusions. No new convention is assumed or required.
Task: combine an admissible integer index, the arithmetic-root sign
restriction, and rejection of a noninteger positive solution.

